{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"pay-review-cycle-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Run the annual pay review cycle","description":"Runs a company's annual pay review in gated steps - budget and principles, increase guidelines, manager proposals and calibration, an equity check and approval, then letters and communication.","category":"hr-operations","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","review","ship"],"role":["operations-manager","executive","founder"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["dataset","text"],"output":["plan","table","report","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"expert","tags":["pay-review","merit-cycle","compensation-planning","calibration","salary-increases"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["build-merit-increase-matrix","allocate-merit-increases","analyze-pay-equity","write-pay-review-letters","design-salary-bands"],"personas":["hr-business-partner"]},"args":[{"name":"company","description":"Headcount, countries, pay structure (bands or not), the rating scale, review effective date, who approves pay, and any collective agreements.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"inputs","description":"What you have for this cycle - budget or the range leadership is considering, pseudonymised pay data with band midpoints and ratings, last year's outcomes and known problems.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"budget","file":"steps/01-budget-and-principles.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/01-budget-and-principles.md"},{"id":"guidelines","file":"steps/02-guidelines.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/02-guidelines.md"},{"id":"calibration","file":"steps/03-proposals-and-calibration.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/03-calibration.md"},{"id":"approval","file":"steps/04-equity-check-and-approval.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/04-approval-pack.md"},{"id":"communicate","file":"steps/05-communicate.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"pay-review/05-communication.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes HR through one annual pay review the way an experienced reward lead runs it: agree what the money is for, turn that into guidelines managers can use, collect and calibrate proposals, test the result for unexplained gaps before anyone signs off, then tell people well. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval, because a budget or calibration decision changes everything after it.\n\n<company>\n{{company}}\n</company>\n\n<inputs>\n{{inputs}}\n</inputs>\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use pseudonymised data only (IDs, not names). Report no group breakdown below five people.\n- Show the working for every cost and percentage; never state a total you did not compute.\n- Do not invent market movement, budgets or band values; mark gaps [X] and ask.\n- Points that depend on employment law, collective agreements, minimum wage changes or tax go to a running list for an employment lawyer or adviser, marked \"confirm locally\".\n- End each artifact with open decisions and what to bring to the next step.","steps":[{"id":"budget","file":"steps/01-budget-and-principles.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/01-budget-and-principles.md","text":"# Step 1: Budget and principles\n\nAgree what this year's pay money is for before anyone proposes a number.\n\n1. Last cycle: what was spent, by rating and position in range, and problems reported (from the inputs; mark gaps).\n2. Pots: merit, promotions, market or equity adjustments, statutory or collectively agreed increases. Recommend whether they are separate and why.\n3. Budget scenarios: two or three totals with the cost of each pot, shown as a percentage of eligible payroll with the working.\n4. Principles: three to five plain statements leadership signs off (for example \"people low in range for their level move faster\").\n5. Eligibility: cut-off dates, new joiners, people on leave (who stay eligible), part-timers.\n\nSections: Last cycle, Pots, Budget scenarios (table), Principles, Eligibility, Questions to confirm locally. Stop for approval."},{"id":"guidelines","file":"steps/02-guidelines.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/02-guidelines.md","text":"# Step 2: Guidelines\n\nTurn the approved budget into guidelines managers can follow.\n\n1. Increase grid: rating by position-in-range band, each cell a target and a range. Cost it against the population distribution and the merit pot; adjust until it fits, showing the working.\n2. Rules: above band maximum, promotions in the cycle, minimum and maximum increases, rounding, lump sums.\n3. Manager pack outline: their budget, the grid, a worksheet with columns to fill, the deadline, and what needs a written reason (any increase outside the cell range or a zero for an eligible person).\n4. Timeline back from the effective date: manager proposals, calibration, approval, payroll cut-off, letters.\n\nSections: Grid (table), Cost check, Rules, Manager pack outline, Timeline. Stop for approval."},{"id":"calibration","file":"steps/03-proposals-and-calibration.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/03-calibration.md","text":"# Step 3: Proposals and calibration\n\nReview managers' proposals before they reach approval. The user pastes the proposals (pseudonymised).\n\n1. Completeness: missing people, proposals outside guidelines without a reason, over- or under-spent budgets by manager.\n2. Patterns: differences in average increase by team or manager for similar ratings and positions in range; outliers with the evidence.\n3. Calibration agenda: the cases to discuss, the question for each, and the facts the group needs. Do not decide them; frame them.\n4. After the user records calibration outcomes, update the totals against budget.\n\nSections: Completeness (table), Patterns, Calibration agenda, Updated totals. Stop for approval."},{"id":"approval","file":"steps/04-equity-check-and-approval.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"pay-review/04-approval-pack.md","text":"# Step 4: Equity check and approval\n\nTest the calibrated outcomes for unexplained gaps before sign-off.\n\n1. Compare increases and resulting pay positions by gender and other groups where data is lawfully held, part-time versus full-time and people on leave during the year, like for like (same level, rating, position in range). Respect the minimum group size.\n2. For each gap: size, likely explanations the data supports, and whether it remains unexplained.\n3. Remediation options and their cost; recommend which to fund from the adjustment pot.\n4. Approval pack: total cost against budget, distribution summary, exceptions with reasons, equity results, decisions requested.\n\nSections: Equity results (table), Unexplained gaps, Remediation, Approval pack, Questions for an employment lawyer. Stop for approval."},{"id":"communicate","file":"steps/05-communicate.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"pay-review/05-communication.md","text":"# Step 5: Communicate\n\nTell people well and close the cycle.\n\n1. Payroll handover checklist: approved file, effective date, back pay if late, checks against the approval pack.\n2. Letter variants needed (increase, promotion, lump sum above range, no increase) and the merge fields, each traced to the approved data.\n3. Manager briefing: how to hold the conversation, what to say about the company-level approach, and how to handle \"why not more?\" and \"why zero?\".\n4. A short company-wide note on the principles and timing, with no individual data.\n5. Lessons for next cycle and a date to start it.\n\nSections: Payroll handover, Letters, Manager briefing, Company note, Lessons."}]}
